  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A plume of anomalous, and deep, moisture will \nget pulled north through the Southeastern U.S. in advance of a \ncold front this weekend. The front will initially move through \nENC on Saturday, then stall offshore before lifting back north \non Sunday. This will occur as a weak area of low pressure rides \nNE along the front. As the low moves off the coast, it will drag\nthe front back south through ENC later Sunday into Sunday \nnight. \n\nModest heating of a moist boundary layer should allow moderate \ninstability to build ahead of the front on Saturday, especially \nacross our southern counties south of HWY 70. It's there where \nwe expect the greatest coverage of showers and thunderstorms to \nbe focused Saturday afternoon into Saturday evening. Guidance \ndiffer some on the progression of the front on Saturday, but \nwith strong ridging centered offshore, it stands to reason that \nthe progress of the front will be slowed, favoring a frontal \npassage during peak heating for our southern counties. Moderate \ninstability plus 2\"+ PWATs suggests a risk of high rainfall \nrates at times. Ensemble guidance suggests 0.50-1.50\" of rain, \non average, in this area. However, where convection is the most \npersistent, rainfall totals may reach as high as 2-3\". This may \nsupport a risk of urban and poor drainage flooding. 3-hr flash \nflood guidance is 3-4\" in this area, which suggests the risk of \nflash flooding is low based on the potential higher-end amounts \nbeing below FFG. It's worth watching, though, given such high \nmoisture content.\n\nOn Sunday, guidance differ on how far north the front will get \nas it briefly lifts back north as a warm front. This doesn't \nappear to impact the ENC from a coverage standpoint. However, if\nthe front were to lift further north, it could open up the \npotential for more instability to be available, which could \nsupport higher rainfall rates and amounts. This could also have \nan impact on temperatures. Regardless, increased low-mid level \nconvergence interacting with a very moist airmass should lead to\na widespread area of showers and isolated thunderstorms. For \nthe area at large, the greatest coverage of showers and \nthunderstorms is expected on Sunday.\n\nIn total, 1-3\" of rain appears likely for much of ENC over the \nweekend. The highest rainfall amounts should be across the \nsouthwestern counties of ENC where there will be the potential \nfor heavy rain both Saturday and Sunday (compared to just Sunday\nfor the northeastern counties of ENC). \n\nThe risk of severe thunderstorms continues to look low due to \nweak shear and weak mid-level lapse rates. However, perhaps a \nfew water- loaded downdrafts of 30-40 mph will occur Saturday \nafternoon when instability is at its peak.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...In the wake of the weekend front, a stiff \nNEerly breeze will develop and peak MON night into TUE. This \nwill lead to building seas and elevated water levels. \nAstronomical tide levels will luckily be on the way down, but \nseas building to 7ft, though relatively choppy periodicity of 7 \nsec, but enough to lead to further beach and dune erosion \nconcerns across the areas with severely diminished dune \nstructures. Strongest winds of 20-25 kt with higher gusts \nexpected MON night into TUE morning. Winds remain out of the \nN-NE into late- week, but speeds diminish to 10-15kt late TUE \nand beyond.",
